  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 307 AM CDT Sat Oct 3 2026\n\nCurrently, troughing is exiting the region to the east with the \nassociated frontal boundary draped across the Ohio Valley and over \nTexas. Upper level northwesterly flow is in place with light and \nvariable flow at the surface as the high pressure is centered just \nnortheast of the state. Water vapor imagery depicts a much drier air \nmass overhead. These dry conditions will persist through the weekend \nand as such, dewpoints were lowered areawide. Conversely, plentiful \nsunshine and low RH values have led to temperatures slightly over \nperforming. Thus, afternoon highs for today were increased above NBM \n90th with mid 70s expected across the forecast area.\n\nLater today, a developing shortwave will traverse the Northern \nPlains. This feature will drag a reinforcing cold front across the \nstate on Sunday, temporarily stifling any rebounding temperature \ntrends. High temperatures on Sunday and Monday will reach into the \nlower 70s for central into southeastern Kansas. Another shot of dry \nair will follow behind the cold front with anomalously low PWATs \nexpected through Monday. Mostly clear skies and zero precip chances \nwill continue through Monday night. \n\nRidging will begin to start building into the region on Tuesday, \nthough another weak perturbation in the pattern on Tuesday night may \nslow the warming trend. But by Thursday, increasing heights will \nlead to afternoon temperatures reaching into the middle 80s by the \nend of the week. A few clouds associated with passing waves are \nexpected for Tuesday into Wednesday, as well as on Friday. \nOtherwise, dry conditions will persist through the end of the \nforecast period.",
